s devotees, who are also called the ‘Devas’. The chief\ndeities are extremely obedient to Vishnu, although they also desire material sensory pleasures, and therefore\nthey are considered deities or equal to God. Rahu tries to attack the Sun and Moon, but\nthey are protected by Lord Vishnu. Due to being afraid of the chakra of Lord Vishnu, Rahu cannot stay in front of the Sun or Moon for more than one Muhurta (48 minutes). When Rahu stops the light of the Sun or Moon,\nthis event is called an eclipse.\nThe scientists of the Earth's attempt to go to the moon\nis like an Asuri attack of Rahu. Undoubtedly, their efforts will fail, because no one\ncan easily enter the sun or moon. Such attempts, like Rahu's attack,\nwill prove fruitless. Then, the fourfold knowledge of the accomplished ones is only that. 4>. Meaning of the word\n\nTatah - from Rahu Graha; Adhastaat - below; Siddha-charana - of Siddhaloka and Charana Loka; Vidyadharanam - and of the Vidyadharas' planets; Kesha - hair; Tavat Matra - only that much distance, 80,000 miles; Eva - undoubtedly. Below Rahu, 10,000 yojanas (80,000 miles) is Siddhaloka, Charanaloka and"
