      "textEn": "69\n\nFrom the jumping of various types of fish; agitated—disturbed; water—in the water; Neeraj—of lotus flowers; Kumud—of Kumudini;\nKuvalay—a flower named Kuvalay; Kahlar—a flower named Kahlar; Neel-utpal—blue lotuses; Lohit—red; Shat-patra-adi—\nA lotus flower with a hundred petals, etc.; Vaneshu—in the forests; Krit-niketanam—those birds that have built their nests;\nEk-vihar-akul—full of unrestrained bliss; Madhur—extremely sweet; Vividh—of various kinds; Swan-adibhih—with vibrations;\nIndriya-utsavaih—those who celebrate the pleasures of the senses; Amar-lok-shriyam—the abode of the gods Beauty; exceeding—to overcome. The gardens of the artificial heavens are befitting of the Paradises' gardens. Those\ngardens, wrapped in trees and vines, lean over the weight of branches laden with fruits and flowers,\nbecause of which they look extremely beautiful. This beauty is attractive to anyone,\nand it is something that makes the mind elated with desire.\nThere are many reservoirs and lakes there, whose\nwater is pure, transparent, and keeps getting agitated by the jumping of fishes, and is decorated with Kumudini,\nKuvalaya, Kahlar, and blue and red lotus flowers. In the lakes, cranes and many other waterfowls roam and happily make noise, which\nlistening to which, the mind and senses are greatly delighted. Where the fear of day and night is observed by the division of time. 11. Meaning of the word\n\nWhere—where; ha vaav—certainly; na—not; bhayam—fear, dread; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; kaal-\nvibhagaih—divisions of time; upalakshyate—is experienced; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; upa-lakshyam—is perceived; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; upa-lakshyam—is perceived; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; upa-lakshyam—is perceived; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; upa-lakshyam—is perceived; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and night; upa-lakshyam—is perceived; aha-ratra-adibhih—because of day and