      "textEn": "\nNeither from their welfare does anything arise, nor from death, but from the splendor of the Lord's radiance. 14. Meaning of the word\n\nNa hi—not; Tesham—of them; Kalyananam—those who are auspicious by nature; Prabhavati—capable of influencing; Kutashchana—from anywhere; Mrityuha—death; Vina—except; Bhagavat-Tejasah—of the divine light; Chakra-Apadeshat—from the Sudarshan Chakra..\n\nThey live very skillfully, and apart from the Sudarshan Chakra of the form of time, Lord Shri Bhagwan,\nthey are not afraid of death by any other means. Meaning: this is the fault of the physical world. In the underworld, each object is arranged in a well-organized manner. There are beautiful abodes, a pleasant atmosphere, and there are no physical or mental ailments. Even then, those who live there have to take another birth according to their actions. Men of low intellect\ncannot understand this flaw of a well-oriented, materialistic civilization.\nNo matter how much one\ncreates situations that please one's senses, in the end, one has to face death. The people of the Asuri civilization try to make their circumstances very pleasant,\nbut they cannot stop death. Due to the effect of the so-called Sudarshan Chakra, their so-called physical\nhappiness cannot remain permanent. In that which enters, the demon wives, mostly, the sacred thread ceremonies flow and fall out of fear.1>. Lexicon"
