्—अत्यन्त विद्वान; अर्थेषु—आत्महित के लिए; न—नहीं; निष्णातः—\nपरम अनुभवी; यः—जो; असौ—स्वर्ग का राजा; इन्द्रः—इन्द्र; यस्य—जिसका; सचिवः—प्रधान मंत्री; मन्त्राय—मंत्रणा के",
      "textEn": "79\n\nIt is not due to karma, but due to his devotion. In other words, it can be said\nthat he is given this position because God wants him to serve them with simplicity and splendor\nin a proper manner. The newly initiated devotee, God remains especially merciful, because from an economic point of view,\nhe becomes poor. This is the grace of God, otherwise, a newly initiated devotee, after attaining prosperity, may forget the service of God. But if God grants wealth to a great devotee, then this\nis not material wealth but should be considered a spiritual opportunity. The devotee forgets God with the material opulence given to the gods, but Bali Maharaj was granted the boon of opulence to maintain devotion to God. Whom Maya had not even touched.\nThat which is beyond the reach of the Supreme Being, by means of which, by the unrestrained desire, by the unrestrained action, the remnants of the body, the three worlds, and the Varuna's noose, are all released.\nAnd having been pierced by the mountain, it is said. 23 ज़a. Meaning of the word:\n\nYat—that; Tat—that; Bhagwat—by Shri Bhagwan; Anadhigata-anya-upayena—which cannot be seen by other means;\nYachnya-chhalena—by means of begging; Apahruta—stolen; Swa-shareera-avasthita—only one's own body remains;\nLoka-trayah—the three worlds; Varuna-pasha-him—by Varuna's noose; Cha—and; Sampratimukta-him—completely bound; Giri-daryam—in the mountain cave; Cha—and; Apaviddha-him—being stopped; Iti—Thus; Ha—indeed; Uvacha—said. When Shri Bhagwan did not find any way to take everything of Bali Maharaj, then he\nunder the pretext of begging for alms, took all three worlds. In this way, only their body remained, but\neven then, God was not satisfied. They imprisoned Bali Maharaj with the Varuna noose and\ntook him to a mountain cave and threw him away.\nAlthough everything of Bali Maharaj was taken away,\nand he was imprisoned and put in a cave, even then, being a great devotee, he spoke in this way. Nuna told that,